# Cline Rules — simple-webpage-hosting-test

## 🧠 Memory Bank
- Always read `memory-bank/activeContext.md` and `memory-bank/progress.md` at the start of every session
- Update `memory-bank/activeContext.md` whenever the current focus changes
- Update `memory-bank/progress.md` whenever a task is completed or a new blocker is found
- Update `memory-bank/techContext.md` when new tools or dependencies are added

## 🐳 Container First
- All development work should happen inside the Dev Container, not on the host PC
- Do not install Python packages, npm packages, or other dependencies on the host
- Use `requirements.txt` or `package.json` to track dependencies inside the container

## 🔀 Git Practices
- Always check `git status` before starting work
- Use meaningful commit messages: `type(scope): description`
  - Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`
  - Example: `feat(auth): add user login endpoint`
- Commit frequently — after each logical unit of work
- Never commit directly to `main` — use feature branches
- Never commit `.env` files, secrets, API keys, or credentials

## 🐍 Python Standards
- Use virtual environments (virtualenv) for all Python projects
- Follow PEP 8 style guide
- Use type hints where possible
- Write docstrings for all functions and classes
- Keep functions small and focused (single responsibility)

## 🌐 Web Standards
- Mobile-first responsive design
- Semantic HTML5
- Accessible markup (ARIA labels where needed)
- Validate HTML and CSS

## ☁️ AWS / Security
- Never hardcode AWS credentials — use environment variables or IAM roles
- Follow principle of least privilege for all IAM permissions
- Use HTTPS everywhere
- Log all errors appropriately

## 📋 Code Review Checklist (before committing)
- [ ] Does it work as expected?
- [ ] Are there any hardcoded secrets or credentials?
- [ ] Is the code readable and well-commented?
- [ ] Are tests written for new functionality?
- [ ] Is the memory bank up to date?
- [ ] Is `.gitignore` covering new file types?

## 🔄 Session Management

### ⚠️ Context Window Alert
- After every **~20 tool calls or exchanges** in a single session, proactively:
  1. Issue a warning: `⚠️ Context window is growing large — saving state and recommending a fresh session soon`
  2. Update all memory bank files with the current state
  3. Suggest starting a new session before the next major task
- Also alert if **more than 2–3 distinct topics** have been discussed in one session — this is a sign the conversation is sprawling and state may be lost
- Never allow a session to end abruptly without saving memory bank state first

### 💾 Memory Bank — Mandatory Save Triggers
Update memory bank files immediately when any of the following occur:

| Trigger | Files to Update |
|---|---|
| Any source file created or significantly modified | `activeContext.md` |
| Any AWS / infrastructure change | `activeContext.md` + `progress.md` |
| Any task or sub-task completed | `progress.md` |
| New dependency, tool, or service added | `techContext.md` |
| Any error or blocker encountered | `progress.md` |
| Before ending any session | All memory bank files |
| Before starting a new major task | `activeContext.md` |

### ✅ End-of-Session Checklist (run before closing out)
- [ ] `activeContext.md` updated: current focus, what was just done, next steps
- [ ] `progress.md` updated: completed items checked off, blockers documented
- [ ] No uncommitted changes that should be staged and pushed
- [ ] Git status clean or pending changes are intentional

## 🚨 Issue Tracking
When a problem is encountered, document it in `memory-bank/progress.md` under "Known Issues / Blockers" with:
- Description of the issue
- Steps taken to investigate
- Current status (open/resolved)
- Date discovered
